  That splits the code cleanly by testability:

  - [1m[38;5;153msession.js[39m — genuinely unit-testable.[0m Get/set/clear round-trips, [38;5;153mnull[39m when empty, the reconcile rule in all three of
    its cases (no prior ID; same ID returned; different ID returned), and the throwing-[38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m fallback from
    Section 1. A fake storage object covers all of it; no browser needed.
  - [1mThe submit handler — not worth unit-testing here.[0m It needs a DOM, which means jsdom or a real browser. For wiring
    this thin, the setup cost exceeds the value. Manual verification in a browser covers it: log in, reload, log in
    again, confirm the second call carries the ID; log in as a different username, confirm the store is replaced.

  [1mThe tooling question.[0m This repo has nothing configured — no test runner, no linter, no formatter, no scripts, and
  [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no dependencies at all. Setting tooling up is cheapest now, before there's code to retrofit, so it's
  worth deciding deliberately rather than defaulting to none.

  My recommendation is [1munit tests only, using Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[0m — it needs zero dependencies, which keeps the
  repo's dependency-free state intact, and it's enough to cover everything in [38;5;153msession.js[39m. Linting and formatting I'd
  take if you want them ([38;5;153mbiome[39m is one dependency and does both), but they're a separate concern from this feature and
  will want to reformat existing files. End-to-end and fuzz testing I'd skip outright: one form, no backend, nothing to
  fuzz.

  Whatever you pick lands in the spec's Global Constraints, so every later task inherits it.
